    "text": "Have you ever wondered how a refrigerator actually makes things cold? It\u2019s not magic, but it is a pretty cool trick involving a special liquid called a refrigerant. Think of this refrigerant as a tiny, busy worker that moves heat around.\n\nThe process starts inside the back of your fridge, where the refrigerant is a gas. A compressor squeezes this gas, making it hot and pressurized. This hot gas travels through coils on the back of the fridge (the part that gets warm to the touch). As it moves through these coils, it releases its heat into the air in your kitchen. This is why the back of your fridge is warm!\n\nOnce the heat is released, the refrigerant cools down and turns back into a liquid. This cold liquid then flows into the inside of the fridge through a special valve. Here\u2019s the key part: when the liquid refrigerant expands into a gas inside the fridge, it needs energy to do so. It steals that energy from the air inside the fridge. Since energy is heat, the air inside gets colder.\n\nThis cold air circulates around your food, keeping it fresh. The cycle then repeats: the gas goes back to the compressor, gets squeezed, releases heat outside, cools down, and steals heat from inside again.\n\nSo, a refrigerator doesn\u2019t actually \"make\" cold. Instead, it moves heat from the inside of the box to the outside of the box. It\u2019s like a heat pump that works in reverse. Every time you open the door, warm air from the room rushes in, and the fridge has to work harder to remove that extra heat. That\u2019s why it\u2019s best to keep the door closed as much as possible to save energy and keep your snacks perfectly chilled!",
